{
  "dom_id": "step-q60652",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/2d97456c31931fc8aa8ee398ad8e4d02c883891ff3abf4236725c6c91c5d5bb0.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(2,-2)$  and $(2,-4)?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $2$ units, so the distance equals $2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/a50056d50fdece1620b695cf02e99a5d94b9c6266b647224cc91a687fa261d2b.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/33c61c33161829ead2a5538d78c5938e54cf1d3db84aab3dfa0d384c01c87161.png)\n\nThe point $(2,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(2,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-2=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
